---
title: Simulations
description: Programmatically animate items and layout changes through DndController for undo/redo, onboarding, and demo sequences.
---

# Simulations

`DndController` exposes two methods for triggering animations programmatically — without any user interaction. Useful for undo/redo flows, onboarding tutorials, or demo sequences.

| Method | Use it for | Mechanism |
|--------|-----------|-----------|
| `animateItem` | Flying a single item to a destination — drop, return, undo | Ghost system (real session, optionally fires events) |
| `animateLayout` | State change where many items reflow — sort, swap, group | FLIP (capture rects → apply state → animate the delta) |

By default neither method fires drop callbacks. `animateItem` accepts `emitEvents: true` if you want a real `onDrop` (style `'drop'`) or `onDropCancelled` (style `'return'`) after the animation. `animateLayout` is a pure visual helper — your `applyState` callback is the single source of truth for data changes.

---

## animateItem

```ts
controller.animateItem(itemId: string, options: AnimateItemOptions): Promise<void>

interface ContainerPosition {
    containerId: string;
    /** Default 0 for `target` containers; required for sortable destinations. */
    position?: number;
}

interface AnimateItemOptions {
    /** Where the item should fly to. */
    to: ContainerPosition;
    /** Where it starts. Defaults to its current location in the slot map. */
    from?: ContainerPosition;
    /**
     * 'drop' (default) — uses ghost-to-target step both same- and cross-container.
     * 'return' — uses scroll-aware return step when destination is the same container.
     */
    style?: 'return' | 'drop';
    /** Fire onDrop ('drop') or onDropCancelled ('return') after the animation. */
    emitEvents?: boolean;
    /** Override the configured drop/return duration. */
    duration?: number;
}
```

Animates a single item flying to a destination through the ghost system. Returns a promise that resolves when the animation completes.

### Example — auto-sort animation

```ts
// Animate item '2' flying to position 0 in the same list.
await controller.animateItem('2', {
    to: { containerId: 'list', position: 0 }
});

// Then update your data:
moveItemToTop('2');
```

### Example — undo a move

```ts
// User moved item '3' from list-a to list-b. Undo it (scroll-aware return):
await controller.animateItem('3', {
    to: { containerId: 'list-a', position: originalIndex },
    style: 'return'
});
moveItem('3', 'list-b', 'list-a', originalIndex);
```

### Example — programmatic move with events

```ts
// Animate AND fire onDrop (your existing handler updates the data model).
await controller.animateItem('task-3', {
    to: { containerId: 'in-progress', position: 0 },
    emitEvents: true
});
// onDrop fires automatically — no manual data update needed.
```

---

## animateLayout

```ts
controller.animateLayout(
    applyState: () => void | Promise<void>,
    options?: AnimateLayoutOptions
): Promise<void>

interface AnimateLayoutOptions {
    /**
     * Items to FLIP. Defaults to every registered draggable except
     * `state.draggedItem` (whose ghost flight handles its own animation
     * during a real drag).
     */
    items?: string[];
    /**
     * When `true`, copies the missing classes from each item's pre-state
     * classList onto the post-state element so class-driven CSS properties
     * (border-radius, scale, background, color, …) transition together with
     * the FLIP transform.
     */
    morph?: boolean;
    /** Override the configured swap duration. */
    duration?: number;
}
```

Animates a state/layout change. Captures item rects before `applyState`, mutates state inside the callback, then FLIPs each item that changed position.

`applyState` is **the single mutation point** — the library reads rects on either side of it. Place all your state changes inside.

### Example — sort all items at once

```ts
const sorted = [...items].sort((a, b) => a.label.localeCompare(b.label));

await controller.animateLayout(() => {
    items = sorted;
});
// Every item glides to its new position.
```

### Example — swap two items between containers

```ts
const oldA = [...teamA];
const oldB = [...teamB];

await controller.animateLayout(() => {
    teamA = oldB;
    teamB = oldA;
});
```

### Example — class-driven shape morph

When the destination container styles items differently (rounded vs square, bigger scale, etc.), pass `morph: true` so the class-list diff transitions with the FLIP transform:

```ts
await controller.animateLayout(applyMove, { morph: true });
// Items glide to new positions AND morph border-radius / scale / colour.
```

---

## Constraints

- **Cannot run while a real drag is in progress.** `animateItem` rejects with an error if `controller.dragging` is `true`. `animateLayout` requires no session and is safe to call any time.
- **Item must exist in the DOM.** For `animateItem`, the item must be rendered in `from?.containerId` (or its current container if `from` is omitted) when the animation starts.
- **`animateLayout` only animates items it can match in the slot map.** If `items` is omitted it uses every registered draggable except the dragged one; pass an explicit list to restrict the set.
- **No events by default.** Only `animateItem` can emit events, and only if you opt in with `emitEvents: true`.

